/*  The model: what a snake IS and what happens to it.

    ★ Nothing here draws, reads a key, or decides when a tick happens. It answers questions and
    hands back new snakes — `moved` returns a snake rather than changing one, because an object
    is a value here and a step forward is a different snake, not the same one edited.

    ★ A direction is two numbers, `row-step` and `column-step`, rather than a named set of four.
    Moving is then addition, and turning is choosing a pair — no dispatch anywhere, and the wall
    check is arithmetic the model already does.
*/

Pull a book on board.
    Define object serpent with () and book:

        /// A snake: where its squares are, head first, and which way it is going.
        Define object worm with (the series of spot body,
                                 the number row-step,
                                 the number column-step):

            /// The square the snake will move out of.
            Bind spot to head:
                Return the first of one's body.
            Done.

            /// How many squares long it is.
            Bind number to length-of:
                Return the number of one's body.
            Done.

            /// Whether the snake lies on a square.
            Bind fact to covers, given (the spot which):
                For each piece in one's body, repeat:
                    If cast piece's same-as on (which), return true.
                Done.
                Return false.
            Done.

            /// The square ahead, whether or not it is on the field.
            Bind spot to ahead:
                Define front as cast one's head.
                Return cast board's at on (front's row + one's row-step,
                                           front's column + one's column-step).
            Done.
        Done.

        /// A snake three long, lying across the middle and heading right.
        Bind worm to starting:
            Define body as a series of spot.
            Insert cast board's at on (4, 10) into body.
            Insert cast board's at on (4, 9) into body.
            Insert cast board's at on (4, 8) into body.
            Return a new worm { the body body, the row-step 0, the column-step 1 }.
        Done.

        /// The same snake, pointed somewhere else.
        ///
        /// ⚠ A snake may not turn back into itself — reversing onto its own neck is a death the
        /// player did not ask for, so the turn is refused and the snake carries on. Every game
        /// does this; it is worth saying WHY rather than letting it look like a bug.
        Bind worm to turned, given (the worm which, the number row-step, the number column-step):
            If row-step is 0 - which's row-step and column-step is 0 - which's column-step,
                return which.
            Return a new worm { the body which's body,
                                the row-step row-step,
                                the column-step column-step }.
        Done.

        /// The snake one step on. It keeps its tail when it is growing, and drops it otherwise —
        /// which is the whole of why eating makes a snake longer.
        Bind worm to moved, given (the worm which, the fact growing):
            Define next as a series of spot.
            Insert cast which's ahead into next.

            Define keeping as the number of which's body.
            If not growing, the keeping becomes keeping - 1.

            Define taken as 0.
            For each piece in which's body, repeat:
                If taken is less than keeping, insert piece into next.
                Increment taken by 1.
            Done.

            Return a new worm { the body next,
                                the row-step which's row-step,
                                the column-step which's column-step }.
        Done.

        /// Whether the snake is still alive: on the field, and not lying across itself.
        ///
        /// ⚠ Asked of the snake AFTER it has moved, so the head is already where it is going.
        /// A snake whose head is on its own body has bitten itself — except that the head is
        /// itself in the body, so what is counted is the head appearing TWICE.
        Bind fact to alive, given (the worm which):
            Define front as cast which's head.
            If not cast board's holds on (front), return false.

            Define seen as 0.
            For each piece in which's body, repeat:
                If cast piece's same-as on (front), increment seen by 1.
            Done.
            Return seen is 1.
        Done.
    Done.
Done.
